**Scope.** The two Codex round-7 commits `167722c62b80d31a1145ed229ff4229e3d619bad` ("Share signal-safe termination across exp04 launchers", +107/−62 = 169 changed lines) and `ed5f2a1146257f45d092f9f74d966185ae2f3689` ("Protect exp04 completion transactions and restore recovery logs", +165/−18 = 183), i.e. `git diff 6dc1821..ed5f2a1`: 272 insertions / 80 deletions over `tools/provenance.py` (+92), `tools/exp04_launcher.py` (94), `tools/exp04_eval_launch.py` (21), `tools/paired_compare.py` (1), `tools/results_table.py` (2), `tests/test_exp04_launcher.py` (73), `tests/test_exp04_recovery.py` (49), `tests/test_results_table.py` (20). They answer the final integrative review (`yaw_aug_xrir_fable_code_final_review.md`: should-fix 1 = eval launcher ignored SIGTERM/SIGHUP; should-fix 2 = `complete_attempt` transaction unmasked; nits 3–6 = uppercase-hex `reviewed_commit` in recovery, recovered log name, producer output modes, `save_dir[:-6]`) per `coder_prompts/round7_prompt.md`. Read before judging: the SOP briefing, the round-7 prompt, the final review in full (its Verified reproductions and 12-step launch checklist), the notebook through the 21:40:36 entry, the Coder transcript `yaw_aug_xrir_2026-09-12_20:40:15_coder_round7.log` and its report (812 passed; "a signal mask alone was insufficient with worker threads so completion also defers Python handlers and protects committed state; log restoration extends A10"), every changed line of the diff, and the current `tools/provenance.py`, `tools/exp04_launcher.py`, `tools/exp04_eval_launch.py`, `tools/exp04_launch.sh` in full.

**Isolation.** All reading, tests and probes ran on a fresh clone checked out at `ed5f2a1` under the scratchpad (`…/scratchpad/r7/r7_clone`; `ckpt/yaw_aug` copied, `ckpt/xRIR_{simple,cyl}_8_shot` and `ckpt/yaw_rotation` symlinked read-only, `checkpoints/` recreated as a directory of symlinks because a bare symlink shows as untracked and would have made the clone's git state `dirty`). The real repository was not modified: `git status` outside `worklog/` shows only the exp_05 Coder's own untracked `tests/test_exp05_params.py`; no file under the real `ckpt/` changed (`cumulative_hours.json` keeps its 19:48 mtime, `alignment_audit.json` 19:02, no `train_inventory.json` cache was written under the real `ckpt/yaw_aug`). GPU work only on GPU 1 (`--gpu 1` / `CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=1`; the suite with `1,0`); both A6000s carried the user's FLAC job (pids 3861098 / 3861659, ~11.5 GB, 100 %) throughout; nothing foreign was killed (I stopped and restarted only my own second pytest process to attach a diagnostic plugin); the training launcher's `full` mode was never invoked — full-mode *functions* were exercised with the real `build_fields`, a stub child and a patched resource gate, exactly as in the previous reviews. This file is the only file I created in the repository.

## Findings

Severity legend: blocker / should-fix / nit / observation. **No blocker, no should-fix.** Both should-fix items and all four nits of the final review are closed on the real path (evidence in "Round-7 items" and "Verified" below). What follows is small.

1. **nit (consistency; Planner's call — changes the `writer` pin if adopted)** — `tools/exp04_eval_launch.py:93-107` (`execute_run`, the tail after the child exits: `p.revalidate` of the 13 318-file identity, ≈ 16 s cached, then `write_completion`). The training launcher's completion transaction is now deferred (`complete_attempt` → `deferred_termination`), the evaluation launcher's is not: a stop signal that lands in the tail aborts a *finished* evaluation. Reproduced (V2, `late`): child exited 0 with valid `per_sample_yaw.json` / `metrics_yaw.json`, SIGTERM during revalidation → `run_t_ABORTED_terminated_SIGTERM` keeping both outputs, `abort.json`, no `completion.json`, log renamed, exit 1. No integrity issue (an aborted directory is never admissible — `run directory contents`), but there is no `finalize` for evaluation runs, so ≤ 70 min of GPU time is lost and the run must be relaunched. This is what the round-7 prompt specified (cycle 2 named `complete_attempt` only). Minimal fix if wanted (≈ 6 lines): wrap lines 93–107 in `with p.deferred_termination():`, set a `committed` flag after `write_completion`, and re-raise without the abort path when it is set, as `execute_attempt` does. Because it changes `tools/exp04_eval_launch.py` it must land before the first confirmatory evaluation and the A9 fill; otherwise the runbook rule is: never signal an evaluation launcher after its child has printed its last `elapsed_min` line.

2. **nit (test hygiene; fix in the next code cycle, 3 lines)** — `tests/test_exp04_launcher.py:522-528` `test_termination_handlers_restore_previous_dispositions` fails **deterministically whenever pytest runs as a background job of a non-interactive shell** (`nohup pytest … &` from a script — how I launched both full passes, and how the Planner runs suites): the shell starts asynchronous commands with SIGINT = `SIG_IGN`, `termination_handlers` respects an inherited `SIG_IGN` (round-6 close-out item 2, re-verified here), so the test's self-sent SIGINT is ignored → "DID NOT RAISE" (V10). Reproduced on the single test: foreground → passed; `bash -c 'pytest … & wait'` → failed; `nohup` in the foreground (SIGHUP ignored only) → passed; a background python from a non-interactive bash reports `{SIGINT: SIG_IGN, SIGHUP: SIG_DFL, SIGTERM: SIG_DFL}`; my diagnostic plugin shows the second pass's pytest process at its very first test with `SIGHUP = SIG_IGN, SIGINT = SIG_IGN` and no later change. It passed 5/5 in isolation, 207/207 in the three launcher files and 218/218 in the ordered preceding files, all in the foreground. It is the only test that depends on the inherited SIGINT disposition (the other in-process self-kills use SIGTERM, which `&` does not ignore; the subprocess signal tests set every disposition explicitly in `preexec_fn`). Not a code defect — it is the launcher behaving as certified under `nohup`-style inheritance — but the test must force its precondition: `previous = signal.signal(signal.SIGINT, signal.default_int_handler)` before the `with` and restore it in a `finally` (or `pytest.skip` when `getsignal(SIGINT) is SIG_IGN`). Until then a green 812 requires a foreground run (or a wrapper that resets SIGINT before importing pytest); a background run reports exactly this one failure.

3. **nit / runbook** — `tools/exp04_launcher.py:657-659` and `:604-606`. When a stop signal is honoured *after* the commit, `execute_attempt` / `finalize_attempt` re-raise `LauncherTerminated`: the launcher exits 1 with a traceback although the attempt is certified (`completion.json`, ledger row, `final` all present — V3). The exit status therefore no longer distinguishes "aborted" from "certified, then terminated"; the runbook's step 10 must say: on exit 1 look for `completion.json` / `final` before triaging as an abort. A one-line `print('CERTIFIED ' + str(attempt), flush=True)` in the `if transaction.get('committed'): raise` branches would make it visible in the launcher log.

4. **nit (accepted residual)** — `tools/exp04_launcher.py:503-516` (`_complete_attempt` rollback). If the log rename-back (`log_path.rename(preserved_log)`) itself raises, the directory rollback below it is skipped and `finalize_attempt`'s `except` then unlinks `completion.json` at the *aborted* path, which no longer exists — the attempt would sit at the recovered name without a completion. Reachable only if the `_ABORTED_` log name is re-occupied or the filesystem fails mid-transaction, the same class as the pre-existing directory-rename-back failure; the forward failures (`occupied_log`, `log_rename`) are tested and verified. Note for the record; no change required.

5. **nit (cosmetic)** — `tools/provenance.py:46-64` `deferred_termination`. (a) It re-delivers only `pending[0]` and restores the *previous* handlers before `os.kill`, so it is correct only inside `termination_handlers()` (a previous `SIG_DFL` would make the re-kill lethal); both call sites are inside it — say so in the docstring. (b) `signal.pthread_sigmask(signal.SIG_BLOCK, previous)` iterates the dict's keys; it works but reads as if a mapping were a mask — `tuple(previous)` is clearer.

6. **nit (test coverage)** — `tests/test_exp04_launcher.py:441-519`: the eval cases assert the child is dead but not the tee sink nor a grandchild of the child; my probe (V1) shows all three are reaped through `reap_process_groups` (child's session = its pgid). Optional assertion.

7. **observation** — the evaluation launcher now writes `abort.json` into the run directory before renaming it (new in `167722c`, mirrors the training launcher); an aborted directory keeps whatever outputs exist plus `abort.json` and is refused by `admit_run` (`run directory contents`) — consistent with the producers' fail-closed admission.

8. **observation (nested handlers)** — `main`, `execute_attempt` and `finalize_attempt` are each decorated with `termination_handlers()`; when an inner context exits it restores the outer `terminate` (fresh `terminating = False`), so a burst signal still pending at that instant can raise a second `LauncherTerminated` from the outer frame while the first propagates, and one landing after the last restore to `SIG_DFL` produces CPython's "Signal N ignored due to race condition" unraisable line (bpo-43406 guard). Harmless: the abort artefacts are written under the mask before either can happen — verified one `_ABORTED_` directory, one log, exit 1 under a five-signal burst in both launchers (V1).

9. **observation** — `apply_umask`'s `os.umask(0); os.umask(mask)` read is process-wide (a concurrent thread creating a file in that window would get 0666); every call site is single-threaded; pre-existing since round 3a, now shared by the two producers.

10. **observation (pins)** — `ed5f2a1` changes `tools/provenance.py`, which is in the `writer`, `entrypoint` and `launcher` closures: the bounded runs record `writer 9f609439…` (15 files) and `entrypoint 2a60f54b…` (the A9 "evaluator" pin) instead of `80f3146`'s `e77da635…` / `66684d81…`; the frozen exp_03 evaluator closure `5ba818d8…` (12 files) is unchanged. Consequently no probe receipt recorded before `ed5f2a1` is valid for it (by design — the rung-6 probe is run at the launch commit), and the A9 pins are computed only once all code is final (checklist step 11).

